00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:06.250
AJA HAMMERLY: So welcome to
a session on Stackdriver.

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:08.494
I'm Aja Hammerly, and
I like it when people

00:00:08.494 --> 00:00:09.660
tweet at me during my talks.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:13.440
My Twitter is @the_thagomizer,
which is spelled exactly

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:14.907
as you think it is.

00:00:14.907 --> 00:00:16.740
And if you come up
afterward, I can tell you

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:18.525
the story behind that.

00:00:18.525 --> 00:00:20.400
Today, we're going to
talk about Stackdriver,

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.330
so let's get a little
bit of show of hands

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:24.550
first-- figure out who all
is in the audience today.

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:27.550
So who's used
Stackdriver before?

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:28.710
OK, awesome.

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:31.350
Who's using it in
production right now?

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:33.810
Smaller set, not surprising.

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:38.160
And who in the audience has a
DevOps, sysadmin, ITOps type

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.110
of title?

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:41.890
OK, smaller group
than I was expecting.

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:44.306
How many people who just think
of themselves as developers

00:00:44.306 --> 00:00:45.000
here?

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.130
Awesome, OK.

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:51.480
So I work at Google as
a developer advocate,

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:55.642
but before I worked at Google,
I worked in ITOps, DevOps--

00:00:55.642 --> 00:00:56.850
whatever you want to call it.

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:59.790
The title seems to
change every week.

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:01.830
And one of my jobs
was racking servers.

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:04.019
This is not actually a
picture of one of the servers

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:08.460
I racked, but many of
mine looked like this.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.890
I also carried a pager
for about three years.

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:12.450
I was half of our on-call team.

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:14.210
Who here has done
the pager thing?

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:15.570
Yeah.

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:18.940
Not necessarily
everyone's favorite task.

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:22.000
And I did a lot of
production issue debugging,

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.050
including connecting my trusty
dev laptop to production.

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:28.750
And I know I'm not the
only one who's done that,

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:31.310
even though it's a bad idea.

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:32.894
So why am I talking
about Stackdriver?

00:01:32.894 --> 00:01:34.934
One of the things I really
like about Stackdriver

00:01:34.934 --> 00:01:36.460
is that all the
tools come together.

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:38.620
Everything is there,
everything is integrated.

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:41.110
When I was doing this
work, before Google,

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:44.080
I was using one tool
for log aggregation,

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:46.210
another tool for
crash reporting,

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:50.230
another tool for application
performance monitoring,

00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:52.270
and then a pile of
tools for monitoring--

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:55.630
Pingdom, Monnit, all
of the above, Nagios.

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:57.850
And whenever something
went really wrong,

00:01:57.850 --> 00:01:59.800
everything would turn red.

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:01.390
And it was really
hard to figure out

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:04.390
what was actually going
on because everything

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:06.640
was alerting simultaneously.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:08.919
It was also annoying when
someone would join or leave

00:02:08.919 --> 00:02:10.419
the team because I
had to grant them

00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:12.430
accounts on all
of those systems,

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:14.720
or take their accounts
away when they left.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:17.104
And Stackdriver does
all of those things.

00:02:17.104 --> 00:02:19.270
It has a lot of that
functionality already built-in,

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:20.600
and it's all integrated.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:22.391
So that's one of the
reasons that I'm super

00:02:22.391 --> 00:02:23.940
excited about Stackdriver.

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:25.771
But I could get up
here and give you

00:02:25.771 --> 00:02:28.270
the marketing pitch for an hour,
and we would all get bored,

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:30.400
including me, so
instead, we're going

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:33.090
to live debug a website using
the Stackdriver toolchain

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:35.280
while I'm standing here.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.184
So I made a simple
Rails website,

00:02:37.184 --> 00:02:39.100
running on App Engine
Flex, which we announced

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:40.720
is now GA as of this morning.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:42.310
Yes, it supports Ruby.

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:46.630
This is my website, Cat or
Not, also known as Tindpurr.

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:50.170
And you can upload your cat,
you can vote on the cats,

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:51.520
and you can see all the cats.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:53.144
And I'll give you a
quick demo of this.

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:01.160
So here's all of
the cats that people

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:04.080
have uploaded to Tindpurr.

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:06.300
As you can see, I have
some performance issues

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:09.516
I baked into this so we
have stuff to look at later.

00:03:09.516 --> 00:03:10.890
But this is what
happens when you

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:15.210
send an email to the entire
team saying, show me your cats.

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:19.480
There's some pretty cute
ones you can look at.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:23.410
And if you wanted to upload
a cat, you could do that.

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:26.080
Standard basic upload form.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:31.226
And for voting on cats, using
the material design UI--

00:03:31.226 --> 00:03:32.350
oh, Chuck Norris and Rambo.

00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:33.420
The picture didn't load.

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:34.503
Let's try a different one.

00:03:36.934 --> 00:03:37.600
So I could vote.

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:38.580
There they are.

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:40.205
So that's all there
is to this website.

00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:48.830
So here's the
basic architecture.

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:50.330
Running it on App
Engine, as I said,

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:52.730
which means I don't care how
many servers I have running,

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:54.650
App Engine takes care of it.

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:56.470
I wanted some sort of
back end processing

00:03:56.470 --> 00:03:58.010
because I don't know
any real websites that

00:03:58.010 --> 00:03:59.850
don't have some sort
of back end processing,

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:02.360
so there's a Pub/Sub
queue set up behind this,

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:04.250
and when someone
uploads a cat, it

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:07.010
calculates which cats
still need more votes

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:08.060
and populates the queue.

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:11.570
And then when you go to vote for
cats, it pulls from that queue.

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:14.540
A little contrived, but,
you know, demo apps.

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:19.630
And then I'm using Cloud SQL
running MySQL as my back end.

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:22.390
So also interesting,
I'm doing a talk

00:04:22.390 --> 00:04:24.370
to a bunch of DevOps/sysadmin
people or people

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:26.770
who like to break
things, I hope.

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:29.200
And if I'm doing that
talk, it's really boring

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:30.700
if there's no load
on my website.

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:33.340
So I spun up Apache
Bench, and is currently

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:35.860
hitting a bunch of
URLs from my website,

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:38.520
just doing get requests--
relatively simple,

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:39.980
but it's better than no load.

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:41.521
And that's running
in the background.

00:04:41.521 --> 00:04:44.050
I'm running it on a
Compute Engine instance.

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:47.290
So let's say I'm taking
Tindpurr to production.

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:50.141
I need some monitoring.

00:04:50.141 --> 00:04:52.140
And the first thing I
need is uptime monitoring.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:54.140
One of my general rules
of thumb with monitoring

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:56.860
is I don't want to monitor
anything that if it goes wrong,

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:58.780
I can't fix it.

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:00.880
So sometimes it's
nice to know things,

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:02.800
but if there's no direct
action you can take,

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:05.117
why do you want to be broken up?

00:05:05.117 --> 00:05:06.450
So let's set up an uptime check.

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:09.290
Actually, I've gotten
an uptime check set up.

00:05:09.290 --> 00:05:12.289
This is going to be a
very demo-heavy talk.

00:05:12.289 --> 00:05:14.080
So I'm just using the
standard uptime check

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:18.030
in Stackdriver for App Engine.

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:21.370
We're going to look at it.

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:24.190
And earlier today, the Virginia
site was failing, but now

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:25.000
it's back up.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.440
I can see exactly where
things are going on.

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:30.070
I can also see a track
of my response codes.

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:35.020
This may correlate to when
I started up Apache Bench.

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:37.630
And you can also see my response
latency, and what's going on.

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:39.100
And this all comes for free.

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:41.502
I just logged into Stackdriver
for the first time.

00:05:41.502 --> 00:05:43.210
They're like, you have
an App Engine app.

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:43.810
I'm like, yes.

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:45.070
Yes, I do.

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:47.880
And this is the
standard uptime check

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:50.040
that comes with App Engine.

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:56.560
So that's the basic
stuff done, but I also

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:58.270
want to check my
database and make sure

00:05:58.270 --> 00:06:01.060
that that's being
monitored, so let's set up

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:02.760
some monitors on that.

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:03.495
Cloud SQL.

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:08.740
So I can see I had a
spike of queries here

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:10.670
and I haven't had
any queries recently.

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:13.270
That's a little suspect,
so I'm going to set up

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:14.320
a new policy for that.

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:24.130
Select Metric rate of
change on Cloud SQL.

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:25.310
Applies to all of them.

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:38.560
And if my read ops decreases
by 15% over 15 minutes,

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:42.307
that seems like something's
probably not right.

00:06:42.307 --> 00:06:43.390
I can only go up to eight.

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:47.630
And I'm going to be notified.

00:06:47.630 --> 00:06:50.622
And I'll be notified as
thagomizer@google.com.

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:59.180
So now I've set up a basic
policy on my database.

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:01.250
If I'm not getting
enough read ops,

00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:03.500
I'm assuming that load has
gone down for some reason.

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:05.146
Likely, something's wrong.

00:07:05.146 --> 00:07:06.770
The other kind of
thing I like to check

00:07:06.770 --> 00:07:08.644
when I'm doing App Engine
is I like to set up

00:07:08.644 --> 00:07:10.025
a policy on the response codes.

00:07:10.025 --> 00:07:11.733
I showed the graph
earlier where a show I

00:07:11.733 --> 00:07:14.270
have a lot of 500
response codes there.

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:15.740
That's a little suspect.

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:18.177
I probably should have
gotten alerted to that.

00:07:18.177 --> 00:07:20.010
So I'll make a new
policy for that, as well.

00:07:30.492 --> 00:07:31.950
And it's applying
to my Cat or Not,

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:34.250
which was the other
name for my app.

00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:36.480
The 500 responses are above.

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:40.120
We'll go with 30 for a minute.

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:40.620
30.

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:45.840
I want to be notified.

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:47.360
And you saw the
blue line show up

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:48.950
that shows where
that is, so I would

00:07:48.950 --> 00:07:52.000
have been notified earlier
today if that had happened.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.850
And this time, I'll have it
notify me with the Console app.

00:07:58.490 --> 00:07:59.090
Name it.

00:08:02.420 --> 00:08:05.710
And now I've got two
basic alerts set up.

00:08:05.710 --> 00:08:08.656
But that's not all
Stackdriver can do.

00:08:08.656 --> 00:08:11.030
One of the many things it can
also do is error reporting.

00:08:11.030 --> 00:08:12.530
And this is one of the
things I really like,

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:14.480
because if you've set
up Stackdriver logging,

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:16.910
it pulls in your logs and
it analyzes them, and finds

00:08:16.910 --> 00:08:19.010
clusters of errors,
and lets you know

00:08:19.010 --> 00:08:20.990
when things have errored out.

00:08:20.990 --> 00:08:23.720
And it doesn't show you the
same error of 10,000 times,

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:24.560
it groups them.

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:27.430
You dive in, you can see
a simple trace of that.

00:08:27.430 --> 00:08:29.150
And it happens to be
that my phone just

00:08:29.150 --> 00:08:32.580
alerted, so let's dig into it.

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:35.030
That may have been planned.

00:08:35.030 --> 00:08:41.387
So if I go to Error
Reporting, let's

00:08:41.387 --> 00:08:42.720
see if the latest error is here.

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:48.790
This is the one
I was looking at.

00:08:48.790 --> 00:08:51.780
I have the Google Cloud
Console app on my phone.

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:57.190
And so I'm getting an undefined
method pick for fixnum 32,

00:08:57.190 --> 00:09:00.720
and that seems ungood.

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:02.750
And I got the whole
stack trace here,

00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:04.370
a bunch of stuff in Rails.

00:09:04.370 --> 00:09:06.167
How many Rubyists/Rails
developers

00:09:06.167 --> 00:09:07.250
do I have in the audience?

00:09:07.250 --> 00:09:08.286
I assume relatively few.

00:09:08.286 --> 00:09:09.160
More than I expected.

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:11.237
Yay, Ruby.

00:09:11.237 --> 00:09:13.070
And so some things I
can do when I get here.

00:09:13.070 --> 00:09:15.170
I can link to the issue
if I had a bug tracker.

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:18.070
I can just put URL in here.

00:09:18.070 --> 00:09:20.970
I can look at the raw
stack trace, which may be

00:09:20.970 --> 00:09:24.600
more useful in some languages.

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:27.270
I can see that it
yielded a 500 message,

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:29.730
and that the first one
was seen 56 minutes ago,

00:09:29.730 --> 00:09:32.850
and it was seen in this
particular App Engine version.

00:09:32.850 --> 00:09:34.260
And I can actually
make it happen

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:35.509
because I know how it happens.

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:42.760
Oh, it didn't happen that time.

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:45.550
But we'll dig into it.

00:09:45.550 --> 00:09:48.690
So now I know that
I have this error,

00:09:48.690 --> 00:09:53.217
and I want to actually
look at it in the logs

00:09:53.217 --> 00:09:53.925
and in my source.

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:03.910
I'll switch over to the logs.

00:10:03.910 --> 00:10:06.810
So I'm just looking at
Stackdriver logging for my Ruby

00:10:06.810 --> 00:10:07.380
app.

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:12.041
If you're using any of our App
Engine or client libraries,

00:10:12.041 --> 00:10:13.290
it will automatically show up.

00:10:13.290 --> 00:10:14.915
To get all the stuff
working with Ruby,

00:10:14.915 --> 00:10:16.470
I just installed
the Stackdriver gem.

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:19.770
I didn't have to actually
do any configuration at all.

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:22.770
It's just straight
drop-in, and a lot

00:10:22.770 --> 00:10:26.280
of the libraries for web
frameworks work that way.

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:29.210
Look at this, and I can
also filter by log level.

00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:34.070
That was the Cloud SQL
database, App Engine.

00:10:34.070 --> 00:10:35.280
I want error or above.

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:39.245
And I want my Ruby app log.

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:45.520
Here's all my errors, and I
can see them here, as well.

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:47.350
This links to Error Reporting.

00:10:50.110 --> 00:10:51.960
There's the error again.

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:53.502
I can mute an error.

00:10:53.502 --> 00:10:55.460
And so at this point, I
have a pretty good idea

00:10:55.460 --> 00:10:58.700
of where this piece of
code that's failing is.

00:10:58.700 --> 00:11:01.380
So this is the point where I
would normally jump into debug.

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:03.590
Stackdriver has a
built-in debugger,

00:11:03.590 --> 00:11:05.959
but it is currently
pre-alpha for Rails,

00:11:05.959 --> 00:11:07.500
and I went to try
it out this morning

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:09.000
and it's not quite
working, so we're

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:13.150
going to actually drive
straight into my editor instead.

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:15.674
So welcome to Emacs.

00:11:15.674 --> 00:11:16.840
So I happened to write this.

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:19.220
This to do-- this is broken.

00:11:19.220 --> 00:11:22.140
And the issue I have here is
that I'm pulling all the votes

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:25.290
from a Pub/Sub queue,
and I'm not actually

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:27.150
refreshing that Pub/Sub
queue except when

00:11:27.150 --> 00:11:29.580
people upload new cats.

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:33.240
So if the queue empties,
I end up pulling null.

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:36.680
And then I try to do a
bunch of stuff with null,

00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:38.560
and that doesn't work so well.

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:45.790
So what I need to do is
if there is no next cat,

00:11:45.790 --> 00:11:50.410
next cat equals
cats cat.all.sample,

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:52.990
which, in Ruby, just pulls
me a single random one

00:11:52.990 --> 00:11:55.750
from the whole
entire list of cats.

00:11:55.750 --> 00:11:57.006
So now I've done that.

00:11:57.006 --> 00:11:58.380
I'm going to deploy
to App Engine

00:11:58.380 --> 00:12:00.910
live because live
demos are awesome.

00:12:00.910 --> 00:12:02.620
And that's going to
take a little bit,

00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:04.194
but I need to hit
Yes first, so we're

00:12:04.194 --> 00:12:05.485
going to go back to the slides.

00:12:16.150 --> 00:12:18.870
So I've talked about some
parts of the Stackdriver suite.

00:12:18.870 --> 00:12:20.620
I've talked a little
bit about monitoring.

00:12:20.620 --> 00:12:22.780
I showed you a
little about logging.

00:12:22.780 --> 00:12:24.790
Logging can also
export to BigQuery.

00:12:24.790 --> 00:12:26.110
I really like BigQuery.

00:12:26.110 --> 00:12:27.610
I frequently give
talks on BigQuery

00:12:27.610 --> 00:12:29.950
because it's amazingly
powerful, and I

00:12:29.950 --> 00:12:32.507
don't have to learn a special
query language to use it.

00:12:32.507 --> 00:12:34.840
So if you have a lot of logs
and you're, like, you know,

00:12:34.840 --> 00:12:36.820
this interface isn't
working for me to, like,

00:12:36.820 --> 00:12:38.986
dig in in the regular
Stackdriver logging interface,

00:12:38.986 --> 00:12:42.430
you can just export to BigQuery
and use SQL or JavaScript

00:12:42.430 --> 00:12:44.602
to search your logs.

00:12:44.602 --> 00:12:46.060
Talked a little
bit about debugger.

00:12:46.060 --> 00:12:47.726
One of the awesome
things about debugger

00:12:47.726 --> 00:12:51.280
is that you can debug
live on production.

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:53.950
And it isn't like your
normal stepping debugger,

00:12:53.950 --> 00:12:56.204
but it still allows you to
debug live on production

00:12:56.204 --> 00:12:58.120
without connecting your
dev box to production,

00:12:58.120 --> 00:12:59.849
which is good in my world.

00:12:59.849 --> 00:13:02.140
But the big thing I haven't
mentioned about Stackdriver

00:13:02.140 --> 00:13:04.000
is that it's cross-cloud.

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:05.500
Most of the features
I've showed you

00:13:05.500 --> 00:13:08.920
work just as well on
AWS as they do on GCP.

00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:09.970
I've shown them on GCP--

00:13:09.970 --> 00:13:13.660
it's GCP Next-- but you can
also do all the monitoring,

00:13:13.660 --> 00:13:16.907
all the logging, all the error
reporting, on AWS, as well.

00:13:16.907 --> 00:13:18.490
Or if you're one of
those people who's

00:13:18.490 --> 00:13:20.281
working in a cross-cloud
environment, which

00:13:20.281 --> 00:13:22.030
lots and lots of
people are now, you

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:25.600
can have all of your information
from both places in one place.

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:29.920
You can set up the exact
same alerts for AWS and GCP.

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:32.110
At Google I/O last
year, I had a database

00:13:32.110 --> 00:13:35.530
where my master
was running in GCP,

00:13:35.530 --> 00:13:37.434
and I had the replica
running in AWS,

00:13:37.434 --> 00:13:39.350
and I had all the same
alerts on both of them.

00:13:39.350 --> 00:13:42.230
And the idea was that it
was a hot swappable setup,

00:13:42.230 --> 00:13:43.990
so if one of my two
providers went down,

00:13:43.990 --> 00:13:47.480
I could switch and not
have as much downtime.

00:13:50.230 --> 00:13:51.990
So other nice thing
about monitoring

00:13:51.990 --> 00:13:53.900
is we have monitoring
that's set up

00:13:53.900 --> 00:13:55.650
for a lot of the common
open source tools.

00:13:55.650 --> 00:13:58.775
This list is not exhaustive,
but I've used it was Mongo,

00:13:58.775 --> 00:14:00.150
I've used it with
Memcached, I've

00:14:00.150 --> 00:14:04.620
used it with MySQL,
Nginx, Redis, RabbitMQ.

00:14:04.620 --> 00:14:07.230
And often, if you have these
and you have the agent running,

00:14:07.230 --> 00:14:08.896
it will look at the
name of the instance

00:14:08.896 --> 00:14:11.550
and the ports that are open,
and will make available to you

00:14:11.550 --> 00:14:13.980
in the UI the metrics that
you are most likely to want,

00:14:13.980 --> 00:14:16.230
because it has a pretty good
idea what you're running.

00:14:16.230 --> 00:14:19.620
If you're running MySQL you have
port 3306, I believe, running

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:22.110
and it's named my
MySQL instance.

00:14:22.110 --> 00:14:24.004
It's pretty sure that
you're running MySQL.

00:14:24.004 --> 00:14:25.920
And if it's wrong, you
can tell it it's wrong,

00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:27.330
but that's really
handy and it saves time

00:14:27.330 --> 00:14:28.538
when you're setting stuff up.

00:14:30.841 --> 00:14:32.840
Also, we have metrics for
a lot of our tool set,

00:14:32.840 --> 00:14:33.549
not surprisingly.

00:14:33.549 --> 00:14:35.465
I showed you some of the
stuff for App Engine.

00:14:35.465 --> 00:14:37.370
You can look at reserved
cores, CPU usage--

00:14:37.370 --> 00:14:38.540
that kind of thing.

00:14:38.540 --> 00:14:40.250
We have our DOS protection.

00:14:40.250 --> 00:14:42.650
You can see if that's
been triggered.

00:14:42.650 --> 00:14:46.790
For compute, CPU utilization,
uptime, read and write.

00:14:46.790 --> 00:14:49.730
BigQuery-- who here
has a really good idea

00:14:49.730 --> 00:14:52.220
how to monitor a
giant data warehouse?

00:14:52.220 --> 00:14:53.620
I didn't.

00:14:53.620 --> 00:14:56.560
And there's stuff we've built
for BigQuery with the BigQuery

00:14:56.560 --> 00:14:57.800
team that makes sense.

00:14:57.800 --> 00:14:59.867
So uploaded bytes,
scanned bytes, how many

00:14:59.867 --> 00:15:00.950
queries you have going on.

00:15:00.950 --> 00:15:03.770
I did a blog post two weeks
ago about different ways

00:15:03.770 --> 00:15:06.320
that you can monitor
BigQuery using Stackdriver.

00:15:06.320 --> 00:15:08.540
Firebase-- super popular,
awesome set of tools.

00:15:08.540 --> 00:15:10.290
You can monitor that, as well.

00:15:10.290 --> 00:15:13.200
And the Stackdriver team and the
Kubernetes and Container Engine

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:15.200
team were working together
to build monitoring

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:17.480
for container-based
systems, which is something

00:15:17.480 --> 00:15:19.604
that a lot, a lot of folks
have figured out exactly

00:15:19.604 --> 00:15:20.188
how to do yet.

00:15:20.188 --> 00:15:22.228
If you want to know more
about all the metrics we

00:15:22.228 --> 00:15:24.090
have available, the URL's here.

00:15:24.090 --> 00:15:25.779
This talk will be
recorded and online.

00:15:25.779 --> 00:15:28.070
Yesterday's talks showed up
before midnight last night,

00:15:28.070 --> 00:15:30.607
so 48 hours or less, hopefully.

00:15:33.470 --> 00:15:34.270
And alerting.

00:15:34.270 --> 00:15:36.160
So you saw me choose
that I could get email.

00:15:36.160 --> 00:15:39.910
I got alerted by
the app on my phone.

00:15:39.910 --> 00:15:42.940
I could also get
an SMS to my phone.

00:15:42.940 --> 00:15:45.010
I've worked at places
that use PagerDuty.

00:15:45.010 --> 00:15:46.120
We connect with PagerDuty.

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:49.230
You can use Slack, you can use
HipChat, you can use Campfire.

00:15:49.230 --> 00:15:50.980
That covers a lot of
the stuff that people

00:15:50.980 --> 00:15:53.860
use for managing their
teams and figuring out

00:15:53.860 --> 00:15:55.960
what's going on in places
that they're looking

00:15:55.960 --> 00:15:58.790
for their notifications.

00:15:58.790 --> 00:16:00.801
So we talked about
error reporting.

00:16:00.801 --> 00:16:02.300
There's lots of
great features here,

00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:03.716
and one of the
other things I like

00:16:03.716 --> 00:16:05.216
is that you can
configure alerts,

00:16:05.216 --> 00:16:06.590
and the alerts
automatically know

00:16:06.590 --> 00:16:08.881
when to stop telling you
about the same thing happening

00:16:08.881 --> 00:16:10.555
over and over again.

00:16:10.555 --> 00:16:11.930
Not only does it
group it, if you

00:16:11.930 --> 00:16:14.060
get over a certain number
of alerts in an hour,

00:16:14.060 --> 00:16:16.539
it shuts up, because it
figures you know at that point

00:16:16.539 --> 00:16:18.080
and you're dealing
with it-- you just

00:16:18.080 --> 00:16:20.210
haven't been able to make
everything go quiet yet.

00:16:22.810 --> 00:16:23.710
Debugger.

00:16:23.710 --> 00:16:25.060
I have played with debugger.

00:16:25.060 --> 00:16:27.760
It's currently
available for Java, Go,

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:32.089
Node is beta, and Python.

00:16:32.089 --> 00:16:33.880
And it's not your normal
stepping debugger.

00:16:33.880 --> 00:16:36.720
You upload your code or
you point the debugger

00:16:36.720 --> 00:16:39.089
at a Cloud repo
or a GitHub repo,

00:16:39.089 --> 00:16:41.380
you figure out the line of
code you want it to look at,

00:16:41.380 --> 00:16:44.170
you set a watch
point, and it will

00:16:44.170 --> 00:16:47.055
catch an in-flight request,
snapshot at that point,

00:16:47.055 --> 00:16:48.430
give you all the
variable values,

00:16:48.430 --> 00:16:50.620
give you the stack
trace, and then

00:16:50.620 --> 00:16:52.780
it'll let the request finish.

00:16:52.780 --> 00:16:55.220
So then you have all the
details about what was going on.

00:16:55.220 --> 00:16:57.219
So if you have that
situation where you're like,

00:16:57.219 --> 00:16:59.290
OK, the stack trace
says it's on that line.

00:16:59.290 --> 00:17:01.810
How the heck is it
getting to that line?

00:17:01.810 --> 00:17:03.730
Why does it have a null
value at that line?

00:17:03.730 --> 00:17:05.380
I don't understand.

00:17:05.380 --> 00:17:06.614
You can set that watch point.

00:17:06.614 --> 00:17:08.530
You don't have to push
an instrumented version

00:17:08.530 --> 00:17:09.579
of the code.

00:17:09.579 --> 00:17:11.260
You can just do it live.

00:17:11.260 --> 00:17:13.849
And depending on exactly the
runtime you're running in,

00:17:13.849 --> 00:17:16.869
it adds some latency
but not a heck of a lot.

00:17:16.869 --> 00:17:19.599
Some of the languages are better
at handling that than others.

00:17:19.599 --> 00:17:23.470
Language runtimes--
they're all different.

